---
title: Creating a Collection Options Creator
id: guide/collection-options-creator
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A collection options creator is a factory function that generates configuration options for TanStack DB collections. It provides a standardized way to integrate different sync engines and data sources with TanStack DB's reactive sync-first architecture."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection options creators follow a consistent pattern:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accept configuration specific to the sync engine"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return an object that satisfies the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CollectionConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" interface"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle sync initialization, data parsing, and transaction management"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optionally provide utility functions specific to the sync engine"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-create-a-custom-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Create a Custom Collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You should create a custom collection when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have a dedicated sync engine (like ElectricSQL, Trailbase, Firebase, RxDB or a custom WebSocket solution)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need specific sync behaviors that aren't covered by the query collection"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to integrate with a backend that has its own sync protocol"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note"}]},{"type":"text","value":": If you're just hitting an API and returning data, use the query collection instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"core-requirements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core Requirements"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every collection options creator must implement these key responsibilities:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"1-configuration-interface","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Configuration Interface"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define a configuration interface that extends or includes standard collection properties:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Pattern A: User provides handlers (Query / ElectricSQL style)\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> {\n  // Your sync engine specific options\n  connectionUrl: string\n  apiKey?: string\n  \n  // Standard collection properties\n  id?: string\n  schema?: StandardSchemaV1\n  getKey: (item: TItem) => string | number\n  sync?: SyncConfig<TItem>\n  \n  rowUpdateMode?: 'partial' | 'full'\n  \n  // User provides mutation handlers\n  onInsert?: InsertMutationFn<TItem>\n  onUpdate?: UpdateMutationFn<TItem>\n  onDelete?: DeleteMutationFn<TItem>\n}\n\n// Pattern B: Built-in handlers (Trailbase style)\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> \n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete'> {\n  // Your sync engine specific options\n  recordApi: MyRecordApi<TItem>\n  connectionUrl: string\n  \n  rowUpdateMode?: 'partial' | 'full'\n  \n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are implemented by your collection creator\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter-specific properties can be returned alongside the core options. TanStack DB ignores these extra properties. Development builds warn only about likely misspellings of a core option, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oninsert"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", when the correctly named option is absent. Missing or invalid required core options still throw."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep adapter behavior in the adapter: returning "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parse"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serialize"},{"type":"text","value":" does not make TanStack DB apply these conversions. Apply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parse"},{"type":"text","value":" before calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"write"},{"type":"text","value":" in your sync implementation, and apply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serialize"},{"type":"text","value":" in mutation handlers. Put "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowUpdateMode"},{"type":"text","value":" inside the returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" object."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"2-sync-implementation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Sync Implementation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each call to the sync function starts a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sync run"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The run owns the callbacks\nand resources installed by that call until its returned cleanup ends them. A\nsync run may make several backend requests or open a longer-lived provider\nsession, so it is not itself a request or provider session."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sync function is the heart of your collection. It must return a cleanup\nfunction for proper garbage collection:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const sync: SyncConfig<T>['sync'] = (params) => {\n  const { begin, write, commit, markReady, markError, collection } = params\n  \n  // 1. Initialize connection to your sync engine\n  const connection = initializeConnection(config)\n  const initialSyncAbort = new AbortController()\n  \n  // 2. Set up real-time subscription FIRST (prevents race conditions)\n  const eventBuffer: Array<any> = []\n  let isInitialSyncComplete = false\n  \n  connection.subscribe((event) => {\n    if (!isInitialSyncComplete) {\n      // Buffer events during initial sync to prevent race conditions\n      eventBuffer.push(event)\n      return\n    }\n    \n    // Process real-time events\n    begin()\n    \n    switch (event.type) {\n      case 'insert':\n        write({ type: 'insert', value: event.data })\n        break\n      case 'update':\n        write({ type: 'update', value: event.data })\n        break\n      case 'delete':\n        write({ type: 'delete', value: event.data })\n        break\n    }\n    \n    commit()\n  })\n  \n  // 3. Perform initial data fetch\n  async function initialSync() {\n    try {\n      const data = await fetchInitialData({ signal: initialSyncAbort.signal })\n      \n      begin() // Start a transaction\n      \n      for (const item of data) {\n        write({\n          type: 'insert',\n          value: item\n        })\n      }\n      \n      commit() // Commit the transaction\n      \n      // 4. Process buffered events\n      isInitialSyncComplete = true\n      if (eventBuffer.length > 0) {\n        begin()\n        for (const event of eventBuffer) {\n          // Deduplicate if necessary based on your sync engine\n          write({ type: event.type, value: event.data })\n        }\n        commit()\n        eventBuffer.splice(0)\n      }\n\n      // A complete initial snapshot is now available.\n      markReady()\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (initialSyncAbort.signal.aborted) return\n      console.error('Initial sync failed:', error)\n      // No usable initial snapshot exists.\n      // Only initial startup owns collection readiness. A later refetch\n      // failure must keep the last ready snapshot usable.\n      if (collection.status === 'loading') markError(error)\n    }\n  }\n\n  initialSync()\n  \n  // 4. Return cleanup function\n  return () => {\n    initialSyncAbort.abort()\n    connection.close()\n    // Clean up any timers, intervals, or other resources\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"3-transaction-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Transaction Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Understanding the transaction lifecycle is important for correct implementation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sync process follows this lifecycle:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"begin()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Start collecting changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"write()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Add changes to the pending transaction (buffered until commit)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"commit()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Apply all changes atomically to the collection state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"markReady()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Signal that a usable initial or recovered snapshot exists"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"markError(error?)"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Signal that initial sync failed before producing a usable snapshot; pass the cause so readiness waits reject with it"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts the transaction and returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". An accepted transaction\nalways applies, in commit order. While an optimistic transaction is persisting,\nthe accepted transaction waits. It becomes visible when that optimistic\ntransaction settles, in the same publication that drops its optimistic state.\nA mutation handler can therefore await its own write without waiting for\nitself. A wrapping sync, such as persistence, returns a promise that resolves\nafter its durable step. A successful "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" must await or return every\ncommit receipt that establishes its result."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The collection keeps the object you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"write()"},{"type":"text","value":" as the row's stored\nvalue. Pass a new object for each update. If your source changes a row object\nin place and writes it again, pass the row's previous value as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"previousValue"},{"type":"text","value":". Without it, the change already overwrote the value the\ncollection needs to publish the update, and live queries can keep the row in\na result it left. In development, the collection throws\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncRowReusedWithoutPreviousValueError"},{"type":"text","value":" for that write when a top-level field\nchanged. The check compares shallow copies, so it does not detect a change\ninside a nested object. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"previousValue"},{"type":"text","value":" for those writes too."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Changes a stored row in place, so it must name the previous value\nconst previousValue = { ...row }\nrow.status = `done`\nwrite({ type: `update`, value: row, previousValue })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For request-scoped writes, pass the request's abort signal to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit(signal)"},{"type":"text","value":".\nIf the signal has already aborted, the transaction is abandoned and the receipt\nrejects with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortError"},{"type":"text","value":". An accepted transaction ignores a later abort: its\nrows apply. To discard a stale page, check the signal before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":". If the\ncaller aborted after the page was accepted, the rows still apply, but reject the\nload with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortError"},{"type":"text","value":" once its receipt resolves. Do not attach one request's\nsignal to a shared stream transaction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If an adapter supplies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unloadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":", release only the acquisition belonging to\nthe supplied options. Release must be idempotent and non-throwing; the adapter\nowns any remote unsubscribe retry. A synchronous "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" throw must clean\nup resources acquired before it throws. Returning a promise transfers ownership\neven if that promise later rejects, so failed acquisitions must remain safe to\nrelease without affecting peers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Race Condition Prevention:"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nMany sync engines start real-time subscriptions before the initial sync completes. Your implementation MUST deduplicate events that arrive via subscription that represent the same data as the initial sync. Consider:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Starting the listener BEFORE initial fetch and buffering events"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tracking timestamps, sequence numbers, or document versions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using read timestamps or other ordering mechanisms"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"4-data-parsing-and-type-conversion","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Data Parsing and Type Conversion"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your sync engine returns data with different types, provide conversion functions for specific fields:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MyCollectionConfig<TItem, TRecord> {\n  // ... other config\n  \n  // Only specify conversions for fields that need type conversion\n  parse: {\n    created_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000),  // timestamp -> Date\n    updated_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000),  // timestamp -> Date\n    metadata?: (str: string) => JSON.parse(str)       // JSON string -> object\n  }\n  \n  serialize: {\n    created_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000),  // Date -> timestamp\n    updated_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000),  // Date -> timestamp  \n    metadata?: (obj: object) => JSON.stringify(obj)                 // object -> JSON string\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Conversion Examples:"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Firebase Timestamp to Date\nparse: {\n  createdAt: (timestamp) => timestamp?.toDate?.() || new Date(timestamp),\n  updatedAt: (timestamp) => timestamp?.toDate?.() || new Date(timestamp),\n}\n\n// PostGIS geometry to GeoJSON\nparse: {\n  location: (wkb: string) => parseWKBToGeoJSON(wkb)\n}\n\n// JSON string to object with error handling\nparse: {\n  metadata: (str: string) => {\n    try {\n      return JSON.parse(str)\n    } catch {\n      return {}\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"5-schemas-and-type-transformations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Schemas and Type Transformations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When building a custom collection, you need to decide how to handle the relationship between your backend's storage format and the client-side types users work with in their collections."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"two-separate-concerns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two Separate Concerns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Backend Format"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - The types your storage layer uses (SQLite, Postgres, Firebase, etc.)"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, JSON strings, PostGIS geometries"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Format"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - The types users work with in their TanStack DB collections"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples: Date objects, parsed JSON, GeoJSON"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schemas in TanStack DB define the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"client format"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (TInput/TOutput for mutations). How you bridge between backend and client format depends on your integration design."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"approach-1-integration-provides-parse-serialize-helpers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approach 1: Integration Provides Parse/Serialize Helpers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For backends with specific storage formats, provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parse"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serialize"},{"type":"text","value":" options that users configure:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// TrailBase example: User specifies field conversions\nexport function trailbaseCollectionOptions(config) {\n  return {\n    // ... getKey, schema, and sync options\n    // The sync implementation applies config.parse before calling write.\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const serialized = transaction.mutations.map(m =>\n        serializeFields(m.modified, config.serialize)\n      )\n      await config.recordApi.createBulk(serialized)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// User explicitly configures conversions\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  trailbaseCollectionOptions({\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    parse: {\n      created_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000)  // Unix → Date\n    },\n    serialize: {\n      created_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000)  // Date → Unix\n    }\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Benefits:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Explicit control over type conversions. Integration handles applying them consistently."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"approach-2-user-handles-everything-in-queryfn-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approach 2: User Handles Everything in QueryFn/Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For simple APIs or when users want full control, they handle parsing/serialization themselves:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Query Collection: User handles all transformations\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch('/api/todos')\n      const todos = await response.json()\n      // User manually parses to match their schema's TOutput\n      return todos.map(todo => ({\n        ...todo,\n        created_at: new Date(todo.created_at)  // ISO string → Date\n      }))\n    },\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // User manually serializes for their backend\n      await fetch('/api/todos', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify({\n          ...transaction.mutations[0].modified,\n          created_at: transaction.mutations[0].modified.created_at.toISOString()  // Date → ISO string\n        })\n      })\n    }\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Benefits:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Maximum flexibility, no abstraction overhead. Users see exactly what's happening."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"approach-3-automatic-serialization-in-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approach 3: Automatic Serialization in Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your backend has well-defined types, you can automatically serialize in mutation handlers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export function myCollectionOptions(config) {\n  return {\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Automatically serialize known types for your backend\n      const serialized = transaction.mutations.map(m => ({\n        ...m.modified,\n        // Date objects → Unix timestamps for your backend\n        created_at: m.modified.created_at instanceof Date\n          ? Math.floor(m.modified.created_at.valueOf() / 1000)\n          : m.modified.created_at\n      }))\n      await backend.insert(serialized)\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Benefits:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Least configuration for users. Integration handles backend format automatically."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"key-design-principles","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key Design Principles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schemas validate client mutations only"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - They don't affect how backend data is parsed during sync"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TOutput is the application-facing type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - This is what users work with in their app"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose your approach based on backend constraints"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Fixed types → automatic serialization; varying types → user configuration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Document your backend format clearly"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Explain what types your storage uses and how to handle them"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more on schemas from a user perspective, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./schemas.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schemas guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"6-mutation-handler-patterns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. Mutation Handler Patterns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two distinct patterns for handling mutations in collection options creators:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"pattern-a-user-provided-handlers-query-standard","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pattern A: User-Provided Handlers (Query, Standard)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The user provides mutation handlers in the config. Your collection creator passes them through."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Handler return values are deprecated. Users should trigger refetch/sync within their handlers."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> {\n  // ... other config\n\n  // User provides these handlers\n  onInsert?: InsertMutationFn<TItem>\n  onUpdate?: UpdateMutationFn<TItem>\n  onDelete?: DeleteMutationFn<TItem>\n}\n\nexport function myCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: MyCollectionConfig<TItem>\n) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n    sync: {\n      sync: syncFn,\n      rowUpdateMode: config.rowUpdateMode ?? 'partial'\n    },\n\n    // Pass through user-provided handlers\n    // Users handle sync coordination in their own handlers\n    onInsert: config.onInsert,\n    onUpdate: config.onUpdate,\n    onDelete: config.onDelete\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"pattern-b-built-in-handlers-trailbase-websocket-firebase","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pattern B: Built-in Handlers (Trailbase, WebSocket, Firebase)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your collection creator implements the handlers directly using the sync engine's APIs:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object>\n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete'> {\n  // ... sync engine specific config\n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are NOT in the config\n}\n\nexport function myCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: MyCollectionConfig<TItem>\n) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n    sync: {\n      sync: syncFn,\n      rowUpdateMode: config.rowUpdateMode ?? 'partial'\n    },\n\n    // Implement handlers using sync engine APIs\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Handle provider-specific batch limits (e.g., Firestore's 500 limit)\n      const chunks = chunkArray(transaction.mutations, PROVIDER_BATCH_LIMIT)\n\n      for (const chunk of chunks) {\n        const ids = await config.recordApi.createBulk(\n          chunk.map(m => serialize(m.modified))\n        )\n        // Wait for these IDs to sync back before completing\n        await awaitIds(ids)\n      }\n      // Handler completes after sync coordination\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const chunks = chunkArray(transaction.mutations, PROVIDER_BATCH_LIMIT)\n\n      for (const chunk of chunks) {\n        await Promise.all(\n          chunk.map(m =>\n            config.recordApi.update(m.key, serialize(m.changes))\n          )\n        )\n      }\n\n      // Wait for mutations to sync back\n      await awaitIds(transaction.mutations.map(m => String(m.key)))\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many providers have batch size limits (Firestore: 500, DynamoDB: 25, etc.) so chunk large transactions accordingly."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key Principle:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Built-in handlers should coordinate sync internally (using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaitIds"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaitTxId"},{"type":"text","value":", or similar) and not rely on return values. The handler completes only after sync coordination is done."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose Pattern A when users need to provide their own APIs, and Pattern B when your sync engine handles writes directly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"row-update-modes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Update Modes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collections support two update modes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (default) - Updates are merged with existing data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"full"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Updates replace the entire row"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure this in your sync config:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"sync: {\n  sync: syncFn,\n  rowUpdateMode: 'full' // or 'partial'\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For complete, production-ready examples, see the collection packages in the TanStack DB repository:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/query-db-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"@tanstack/query-db-collection"}]}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Pattern A: User-provided handlers with full refetch strategy"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/trailbase-db-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"@tanstack/trailbase-db-collection"}]}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Pattern B: Built-in handlers with ID-based tracking"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/electric-db-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"@tanstack/electric-db-collection"}]}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Pattern A: Transaction ID tracking with complex sync protocols"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/rxdb-db-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"@tanstack/rxdb-db-collection"}]}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Pattern B: Built-in handlers that bridge "},{"type":"link","href":"https://rxdb.info","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RxDB"}]},{"type":"text","value":" change streams into TanStack DB's sync lifecycle"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"key-lessons-from-production-collections","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key Lessons from Production Collections"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From Query Collection:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Simplest approach: Full refetch after mutations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best for: APIs without real-time sync"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pattern: User provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":" handlers"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From Trailbase Collection:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shows ID-based optimistic state management"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handles provider batch limits (chunking large operations)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pattern: Collection provides mutation handlers using record API"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From Electric Collection:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complex transaction ID tracking for distributed sync"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demonstrates advanced deduplication techniques"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shows how to wrap user handlers with sync coordination"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From RxDB Collection:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uses RxDB's built-in queries and change streams"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RxCollection.$"},{"type":"text","value":" to subscribe to inserts/updates/deletes and forward them to TanStack DB with begin-write-commit"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implements built-in mutation handlers (onInsert, onUpdate, onDelete) that call RxDB APIs (bulkUpsert, incrementalPatch, bulkRemove)"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complete-example-websocket-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete Example: WebSocket Collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's a complete example of a WebSocket-based collection options creator that demonstrates the full round-trip flow:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client sends transaction with all mutations batched together"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server processes the transaction and may modify the data (validation, timestamps, etc.)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server sends back acknowledgment and the actual processed data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client waits for this round-trip before dropping optimistic state"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type {\n  CollectionConfig,\n  SyncConfig,\n  InsertMutationFnParams,\n  UpdateMutationFnParams,\n  DeleteMutationFnParams,\n  UtilsRecord\n} from '@tanstack/db'\n\ninterface WebSocketMessage<T> {\n  type: 'insert' | 'update' | 'delete' | 'sync' | 'transaction' | 'ack'\n  data?: T | T[]\n  mutations?: Array<{\n    type: 'insert' | 'update' | 'delete'\n    data: T\n    id?: string\n  }>\n  transactionId?: string\n  id?: string\n}\n\ninterface WebSocketCollectionConfig<TItem extends object>\n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete' | 'sync'> {\n  url: string\n  reconnectInterval?: number\n  \n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are handled by the WebSocket connection\n  // Users don't provide these handlers\n}\n\ninterface WebSocketUtils extends UtilsRecord {\n  reconnect: () => void\n  getConnectionState: () => 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'connecting'\n}\n\nexport function webSocketCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: WebSocketCollectionConfig<TItem>\n): CollectionConfig<TItem> & { utils: WebSocketUtils } {\n  let ws: WebSocket | null = null\n  let reconnectTimer: NodeJS.Timeout | null = null\n  let connectionState: 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'connecting' = 'disconnected'\n  \n  // Track pending transactions awaiting acknowledgment\n  const pendingTransactions = new Map<string, {\n    resolve: () => void\n    reject: (error: Error) => void\n    timeout: NodeJS.Timeout\n  }>()\n  \n  const sync: SyncConfig<TItem>['sync'] = (params) => {\n    const { begin, write, commit, markReady } = params\n    \n    function connect() {\n      connectionState = 'connecting'\n      ws = new WebSocket(config.url)\n      \n      ws.onopen = () => {\n        connectionState = 'connected'\n        // Request initial sync\n        ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'sync' }))\n      }\n      \n      ws.onmessage = (event) => {\n        const message: WebSocketMessage<TItem> = JSON.parse(event.data)\n        \n        switch (message.type) {\n          case 'sync':\n            // Initial sync with array of items\n            begin()\n            if (Array.isArray(message.data)) {\n              for (const item of message.data) {\n                write({ type: 'insert', value: item })\n              }\n            }\n            commit()\n            markReady()\n            break\n            \n          case 'insert':\n          case 'update':\n          case 'delete':\n            // Real-time updates from other clients\n            begin()\n            write({ \n              type: message.type, \n              value: message.data as TItem \n            })\n            commit()\n            break\n            \n          case 'ack':\n            // Server acknowledged our transaction\n            if (message.transactionId) {\n              const pending = pendingTransactions.get(message.transactionId)\n              if (pending) {\n                clearTimeout(pending.timeout)\n                pendingTransactions.delete(message.transactionId)\n                pending.resolve()\n              }\n            }\n            break\n            \n          case 'transaction':\n            // Server sending back the actual data after processing our transaction\n            if (message.mutations) {\n              begin()\n              for (const mutation of message.mutations) {\n                write({\n                  type: mutation.type,\n                  value: mutation.data\n                })\n              }\n              commit()\n            }\n            break\n        }\n      }\n      \n      ws.onerror = (error) => {\n        console.error('WebSocket error:', error)\n        connectionState = 'disconnected'\n      }\n      \n      ws.onclose = () => {\n        connectionState = 'disconnected'\n        // Auto-reconnect\n        if (!reconnectTimer) {\n          reconnectTimer = setTimeout(() => {\n            reconnectTimer = null\n            connect()\n          }, config.reconnectInterval || 5000)\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Start connection\n    connect()\n    \n    // Return cleanup function\n    return () => {\n      if (reconnectTimer) {\n        clearTimeout(reconnectTimer)\n        reconnectTimer = null\n      }\n      if (ws) {\n        ws.close()\n        ws = null\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Helper function to send transaction and wait for server acknowledgment\n  const sendTransaction = async (\n    params: InsertMutationFnParams<TItem> | UpdateMutationFnParams<TItem> | DeleteMutationFnParams<TItem>\n  ): Promise<void> => {\n    if (ws?.readyState !== WebSocket.OPEN) {\n      throw new Error('WebSocket not connected')\n    }\n    \n    const transactionId = crypto.randomUUID()\n    \n    // Convert all mutations in the transaction to the wire format\n    const mutations = params.transaction.mutations.map(mutation => ({\n      type: mutation.type,\n      id: mutation.key,\n      data: mutation.type === 'delete' ? undefined : \n           mutation.type === 'update' ? mutation.changes : \n           mutation.modified\n    }))\n    \n    // Send the entire transaction at once\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({\n      type: 'transaction',\n      transactionId,\n      mutations\n    }))\n    \n    // Wait for server acknowledgment\n    return new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {\n      const timeout = setTimeout(() => {\n        pendingTransactions.delete(transactionId)\n        reject(new Error(`Transaction ${transactionId} timed out`))\n      }, 10000) // 10 second timeout\n      \n      pendingTransactions.set(transactionId, {\n        resolve,\n        reject,\n        timeout\n      })\n    })\n  }\n  \n  // All mutation handlers use the same transaction sender\n  // Handlers wait for server acknowledgment before completing\n  const onInsert = async (params: InsertMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n    // Handler completes after server confirms the transaction\n  }\n\n  const onUpdate = async (params: UpdateMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n  }\n\n  const onDelete = async (params: DeleteMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n  }\n  \n  return {\n    id: config.id,\n    schema: config.schema,\n    getKey: config.getKey,\n    sync: { sync },\n    onInsert,\n    onUpdate,\n    onDelete,\n    utils: {\n      reconnect: () => {\n        if (ws) ws.close()\n        connect()\n      },\n      getConnectionState: () => connectionState\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"usage-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage Example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { DbClient, collectionOptions } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { webSocketCollectionOptions } from './websocket-collection'\n\nconst db = new DbClient()\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions('todos', () =>\n  webSocketCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'todos',\n    url: 'ws://localhost:8080/todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    // Note: No onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete - handled by WebSocket automatically\n  })\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n\n// Use the collection\ntodos.insert({ id: '1', text: 'Buy milk', completed: false })\n\n// Access utilities\ntodos.utils.getConnectionState() // 'connected'\ntodos.utils.reconnect() // Force reconnect","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"advanced-managing-optimistic-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced: Managing Optimistic State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A critical challenge in sync-first apps is knowing when to drop optimistic state. When a user makes a change:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The UI updates immediately (optimistic update)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mutation is sent to the backend"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The backend processes and persists the change"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The change syncs back to the client"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optimistic state should be dropped in favor of the synced data"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The key question is: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do you know when step 4 is complete?"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"strategy-1-built-in-provider-methods-recommended","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strategy 1: Built-in Provider Methods (Recommended)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many providers offer built-in methods to wait for sync completion:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Firebase\nawait waitForPendingWrites(firestore)\n\n// Custom WebSocket\nawait websocket.waitForAck(transactionId)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"strategy-2-transaction-id-tracking-electricsql","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strategy 2: Transaction ID Tracking (ElectricSQL)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ElectricSQL returns transaction IDs that you can track:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Track seen transaction IDs\nconst seenTxids = new Store<Set<number>>(new Set())\n\n// In sync, track txids from incoming messages\nif (message.headers.txids) {\n  message.headers.txids.forEach(txid => {\n    seenTxids.setState(prev => new Set([...prev, txid]))\n  })\n}\n\n// Electric-specific: expose awaitTxId through collection.utils so handlers\n// can coordinate txid-based sync before they complete\nconst wrappedOnInsert = async (params) => {\n  // The user handler persists the mutation, then calls\n  // await params.collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid) internally.\n  await config.onInsert!(params)\n}\n\n// Utility function to wait for a txid\nconst awaitTxId = (txId: number): Promise<boolean> => {\n  if (seenTxids.state.has(txId)) return Promise.resolve(true)\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const unsubscribe = seenTxids.subscribe(() => {\n      if (seenTxids.state.has(txId)) {\n        unsubscribe()\n        resolve(true)\n      }\n    })\n  })\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"strategy-3-id-based-tracking-trailbase","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strategy 3: ID-Based Tracking (Trailbase)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trailbase tracks when specific record IDs have been synced:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Track synced IDs with timestamps\nconst seenIds = new Store(new Map<string, number>())\n\n// In sync, mark IDs as seen\nwrite({ type: 'insert', value: item })\nseenIds.setState(prev => new Map(prev).set(item.id, Date.now()))\n\n// Wait for specific IDs after mutations\nconst wrappedOnInsert = async (params) => {\n  const ids = await config.recordApi.createBulk(items)\n\n  // Wait for all IDs to be synced back before handler completes\n  await awaitIds(ids)\n}\n\nconst awaitIds = (ids: string[]): Promise<void> => {\n  const allSynced = ids.every(id => seenIds.state.has(id))\n  if (allSynced) return Promise.resolve()\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const unsubscribe = seenIds.subscribe((state) => {\n      if (ids.every(id => state.has(id))) {\n        unsubscribe()\n        resolve()\n      }\n    })\n  })\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"strategy-4-version-timestamp-tracking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strategy 4: Version/Timestamp Tracking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Track version numbers or timestamps to detect when data is fresh:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Track latest sync timestamp\nlet lastSyncTime = 0\n\n// In mutations, record when the operation was sent\nconst wrappedOnUpdate = async (params) => {\n  const mutationTime = Date.now()\n  await config.onUpdate(params)\n\n  // Wait for sync to catch up before handler completes\n  await waitForSync(mutationTime)\n}\n\nconst waitForSync = (afterTime: number): Promise<void> => {\n  if (lastSyncTime > afterTime) return Promise.resolve()\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const check = setInterval(() => {\n      if (lastSyncTime > afterTime) {\n        clearInterval(check)\n        resolve()\n      }\n    }, 100)\n  })\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"strategy-5-refetch-query-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strategy 5: Refetch (Query Collection)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The query collection pattern has users refetch after mutations:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Pattern A: User provides handlers and manages refetch\nexport function queryCollectionOptions<TItem>(config) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n\n    // User provides handlers and they handle refetch themselves\n    onInsert: config.onInsert,  // User calls collection.utils.refetch() in their handler\n    onUpdate: config.onUpdate,\n    onDelete: config.onDelete,\n\n    utils: {\n      refetch: () => {\n        // Refetch implementation that syncs fresh data\n        // automatically dropping optimistic state\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Usage: User refetches in their handler\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map(m => m.modified))\n      // User explicitly triggers refetch\n      await collection.utils.refetch()\n      // Prevent the pre-1.0 compatibility wrapper from refetching again.\n      return { refetch: false }\n    }\n  })\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"choosing-a-strategy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a Strategy"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in Methods"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Best when your provider offers sync completion APIs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transaction IDs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Best when your backend provides reliable transaction tracking"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID-Based"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Good for systems where each mutation returns the affected IDs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full Refetch"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Simplest but least efficient; good for small datasets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Version/Timestamp"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Works when your sync includes reliable ordering information"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"implementation-tips","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implementation Tips"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Always wait for sync"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in your mutation handlers to ensure optimistic state is properly managed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle timeouts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Don't wait forever for sync confirmation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clean up tracking data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Remove old txids/IDs to prevent memory leaks"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide utilities"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Export functions like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaitTxId"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaitSync"},{"type":"text","value":" for advanced use cases"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"best-practices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best Practices"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Report initial sync status"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady()"},{"type":"text","value":" after a usable snapshot, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markError(error)"},{"type":"text","value":" if initial sync fails"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recover explicitly"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - After an error, call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady()"},{"type":"text","value":" only when a later sync has produced a usable snapshot"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clean up resources"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Return a cleanup function from sync to prevent memory leaks"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batch operations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Use begin/commit to batch multiple changes for better performance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Race Conditions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Start listeners before initial fetch and buffer events"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type safety"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Use TypeScript generics to maintain type safety throughout"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide utilities"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Export sync-engine-specific utilities for advanced use cases"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handler return values are deprecated"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Mutation handlers should coordinate sync internally (via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await"},{"type":"text","value":") rather than returning values. For Electric collections, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of returning the txid"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"testing-your-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing Your Collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test your collection options creator with:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unit tests"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Test sync logic, data transformations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Integration tests"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Test with real sync engine"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error scenarios"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Connection failures, invalid data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Large datasets, frequent updates"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"conclusion","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conclusion"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating a collection options creator allows you to integrate any sync engine with TanStack DB's powerful sync-first architecture. Follow the patterns shown here, and you'll have a robust, type-safe integration that provides excellent developer experience."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2},{"id":"when-to-create-a-custom-collection","text":"When to Create a Custom Collection","level":2},{"id":"core-requirements","text":"Core Requirements","level":2},{"id":"1-configuration-interface","text":"1. Configuration Interface","level":3},{"id":"2-sync-implementation","text":"2. Sync Implementation","level":3},{"id":"3-transaction-lifecycle","text":"3. Transaction Lifecycle","level":3},{"id":"4-data-parsing-and-type-conversion","text":"4. Data Parsing and Type Conversion","level":3},{"id":"5-schemas-and-type-transformations","text":"5. Schemas and Type Transformations","level":3},{"id":"two-separate-concerns","text":"Two Separate Concerns","level":4},{"id":"approach-1-integration-provides-parse-serialize-helpers","text":"Approach 1: Integration Provides Parse/Serialize Helpers","level":4},{"id":"approach-2-user-handles-everything-in-queryfn-handlers","text":"Approach 2: User Handles Everything in QueryFn/Handlers","level":4},{"id":"approach-3-automatic-serialization-in-handlers","text":"Approach 3: Automatic Serialization in Handlers","level":4},{"id":"key-design-principles","text":"Key Design Principles","level":4},{"id":"6-mutation-handler-patterns","text":"6. Mutation Handler Patterns","level":3},{"id":"pattern-a-user-provided-handlers-query-standard","text":"Pattern A: User-Provided Handlers (Query, Standard)","level":4},{"id":"pattern-b-built-in-handlers-trailbase-websocket-firebase","text":"Pattern B: Built-in Handlers (Trailbase, WebSocket, Firebase)","level":4},{"id":"row-update-modes","text":"Row Update Modes","level":2},{"id":"production-examples","text":"Production Examples","level":2},{"id":"key-lessons-from-production-collections","text":"Key Lessons from Production Collections","level":3},{"id":"complete-example-websocket-collection","text":"Complete Example: WebSocket Collection","level":2},{"id":"usage-example","text":"Usage Example","level":2},{"id":"advanced-managing-optimistic-state","text":"Advanced: Managing Optimistic State","level":2},{"id":"strategy-1-built-in-provider-methods-recommended","text":"Strategy 1: Built-in Provider Methods (Recommended)","level":3},{"id":"strategy-2-transaction-id-tracking-electricsql","text":"Strategy 2: Transaction ID Tracking (ElectricSQL)","level":3},{"id":"strategy-3-id-based-tracking-trailbase","text":"Strategy 3: ID-Based Tracking (Trailbase)","level":3},{"id":"strategy-4-version-timestamp-tracking","text":"Strategy 4: Version/Timestamp Tracking","level":3},{"id":"strategy-5-refetch-query-collection","text":"Strategy 5: Refetch (Query Collection)","level":3},{"id":"choosing-a-strategy","text":"Choosing a Strategy","level":3},{"id":"implementation-tips","text":"Implementation Tips","level":3},{"id":"best-practices","text":"Best Practices","level":2},{"id":"testing-your-collection","text":"Testing Your Collection","level":2},{"id":"conclusion","text":"Conclusion","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"A collection options creator is a factory function that generates configuration options for TanStack DB collections. It provides a standardized way to integrate different sync engines and data sources with TanStack DB's reactive sync-first architecture.\n\n## Overview\n\nCollection options creators follow a consistent pattern:\n1. Accept configuration specific to the sync engine\n2. Return an object that satisfies the `CollectionConfig` interface\n3. Handle sync initialization, data parsing, and transaction management\n4. Optionally provide utility functions specific to the sync engine\n\n## When to Create a Custom Collection\n\nYou should create a custom collection when:\n- You have a dedicated sync engine (like ElectricSQL, Trailbase, Firebase, RxDB or a custom WebSocket solution)\n- You need specific sync behaviors that aren't covered by the query collection\n- You want to integrate with a backend that has its own sync protocol\n\n**Note**: If you're just hitting an API and returning data, use the query collection instead.\n\n## Core Requirements\n\nEvery collection options creator must implement these key responsibilities:\n\n### 1. Configuration Interface\n\nDefine a configuration interface that extends or includes standard collection properties:\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern A: User provides handlers (Query / ElectricSQL style)\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> {\n  // Your sync engine specific options\n  connectionUrl: string\n  apiKey?: string\n  \n  // Standard collection properties\n  id?: string\n  schema?: StandardSchemaV1\n  getKey: (item: TItem) => string | number\n  sync?: SyncConfig<TItem>\n  \n  rowUpdateMode?: 'partial' | 'full'\n  \n  // User provides mutation handlers\n  onInsert?: InsertMutationFn<TItem>\n  onUpdate?: UpdateMutationFn<TItem>\n  onDelete?: DeleteMutationFn<TItem>\n}\n\n// Pattern B: Built-in handlers (Trailbase style)\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> \n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete'> {\n  // Your sync engine specific options\n  recordApi: MyRecordApi<TItem>\n  connectionUrl: string\n  \n  rowUpdateMode?: 'partial' | 'full'\n  \n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are implemented by your collection creator\n}\n```\n\nAdapter-specific properties can be returned alongside the core options. TanStack DB ignores these extra properties. Development builds warn only about likely misspellings of a core option, such as `oninsert` for `onInsert`, when the correctly named option is absent. Missing or invalid required core options still throw.\n\nKeep adapter behavior in the adapter: returning `parse` or `serialize` does not make TanStack DB apply these conversions. Apply `parse` before calling `write` in your sync implementation, and apply `serialize` in mutation handlers. Put `rowUpdateMode` inside the returned `sync` object.\n\n### 2. Sync Implementation\n\nEach call to the sync function starts a **sync run**. The run owns the callbacks\nand resources installed by that call until its returned cleanup ends them. A\nsync run may make several backend requests or open a longer-lived provider\nsession, so it is not itself a request or provider session.\n\nThe sync function is the heart of your collection. It must return a cleanup\nfunction for proper garbage collection:\n\n```typescript\nconst sync: SyncConfig<T>['sync'] = (params) => {\n  const { begin, write, commit, markReady, markError, collection } = params\n  \n  // 1. Initialize connection to your sync engine\n  const connection = initializeConnection(config)\n  const initialSyncAbort = new AbortController()\n  \n  // 2. Set up real-time subscription FIRST (prevents race conditions)\n  const eventBuffer: Array<any> = []\n  let isInitialSyncComplete = false\n  \n  connection.subscribe((event) => {\n    if (!isInitialSyncComplete) {\n      // Buffer events during initial sync to prevent race conditions\n      eventBuffer.push(event)\n      return\n    }\n    \n    // Process real-time events\n    begin()\n    \n    switch (event.type) {\n      case 'insert':\n        write({ type: 'insert', value: event.data })\n        break\n      case 'update':\n        write({ type: 'update', value: event.data })\n        break\n      case 'delete':\n        write({ type: 'delete', value: event.data })\n        break\n    }\n    \n    commit()\n  })\n  \n  // 3. Perform initial data fetch\n  async function initialSync() {\n    try {\n      const data = await fetchInitialData({ signal: initialSyncAbort.signal })\n      \n      begin() // Start a transaction\n      \n      for (const item of data) {\n        write({\n          type: 'insert',\n          value: item\n        })\n      }\n      \n      commit() // Commit the transaction\n      \n      // 4. Process buffered events\n      isInitialSyncComplete = true\n      if (eventBuffer.length > 0) {\n        begin()\n        for (const event of eventBuffer) {\n          // Deduplicate if necessary based on your sync engine\n          write({ type: event.type, value: event.data })\n        }\n        commit()\n        eventBuffer.splice(0)\n      }\n\n      // A complete initial snapshot is now available.\n      markReady()\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (initialSyncAbort.signal.aborted) return\n      console.error('Initial sync failed:', error)\n      // No usable initial snapshot exists.\n      // Only initial startup owns collection readiness. A later refetch\n      // failure must keep the last ready snapshot usable.\n      if (collection.status === 'loading') markError(error)\n    }\n  }\n\n  initialSync()\n  \n  // 4. Return cleanup function\n  return () => {\n    initialSyncAbort.abort()\n    connection.close()\n    // Clean up any timers, intervals, or other resources\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Transaction Lifecycle\n\nUnderstanding the transaction lifecycle is important for correct implementation.\n\nThe sync process follows this lifecycle:\n\n1. **begin()** - Start collecting changes\n2. **write()** - Add changes to the pending transaction (buffered until commit)\n3. **commit()** - Apply all changes atomically to the collection state\n4. **markReady()** - Signal that a usable initial or recovered snapshot exists\n5. **markError(error?)** - Signal that initial sync failed before producing a usable snapshot; pass the cause so readiness waits reject with it\n\n`commit()` accepts the transaction and returns `true`. An accepted transaction\nalways applies, in commit order. While an optimistic transaction is persisting,\nthe accepted transaction waits. It becomes visible when that optimistic\ntransaction settles, in the same publication that drops its optimistic state.\nA mutation handler can therefore await its own write without waiting for\nitself. A wrapping sync, such as persistence, returns a promise that resolves\nafter its durable step. A successful `loadSubset` must await or return every\ncommit receipt that establishes its result.\n\nThe collection keeps the object you pass to `write()` as the row's stored\nvalue. Pass a new object for each update. If your source changes a row object\nin place and writes it again, pass the row's previous value as\n`previousValue`. Without it, the change already overwrote the value the\ncollection needs to publish the update, and live queries can keep the row in\na result it left. In development, the collection throws\n`SyncRowReusedWithoutPreviousValueError` for that write when a top-level field\nchanged. The check compares shallow copies, so it does not detect a change\ninside a nested object. Pass `previousValue` for those writes too.\n\n```ts\n// Changes a stored row in place, so it must name the previous value\nconst previousValue = { ...row }\nrow.status = `done`\nwrite({ type: `update`, value: row, previousValue })\n```\n\nFor request-scoped writes, pass the request's abort signal to `commit(signal)`.\nIf the signal has already aborted, the transaction is abandoned and the receipt\nrejects with `AbortError`. An accepted transaction ignores a later abort: its\nrows apply. To discard a stale page, check the signal before `commit()`. If the\ncaller aborted after the page was accepted, the rows still apply, but reject the\nload with `AbortError` once its receipt resolves. Do not attach one request's\nsignal to a shared stream transaction.\n\nIf an adapter supplies `unloadSubset`, release only the acquisition belonging to\nthe supplied options. Release must be idempotent and non-throwing; the adapter\nowns any remote unsubscribe retry. A synchronous `loadSubset` throw must clean\nup resources acquired before it throws. Returning a promise transfers ownership\neven if that promise later rejects, so failed acquisitions must remain safe to\nrelease without affecting peers.\n\n**Race Condition Prevention:**\nMany sync engines start real-time subscriptions before the initial sync completes. Your implementation MUST deduplicate events that arrive via subscription that represent the same data as the initial sync. Consider:\n- Starting the listener BEFORE initial fetch and buffering events\n- Tracking timestamps, sequence numbers, or document versions\n- Using read timestamps or other ordering mechanisms\n\n### 4. Data Parsing and Type Conversion\n\nIf your sync engine returns data with different types, provide conversion functions for specific fields:\n\n```typescript\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem, TRecord> {\n  // ... other config\n  \n  // Only specify conversions for fields that need type conversion\n  parse: {\n    created_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000),  // timestamp -> Date\n    updated_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000),  // timestamp -> Date\n    metadata?: (str: string) => JSON.parse(str)       // JSON string -> object\n  }\n  \n  serialize: {\n    created_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000),  // Date -> timestamp\n    updated_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000),  // Date -> timestamp  \n    metadata?: (obj: object) => JSON.stringify(obj)                 // object -> JSON string\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Type Conversion Examples:**\n```typescript\n// Firebase Timestamp to Date\nparse: {\n  createdAt: (timestamp) => timestamp?.toDate?.() || new Date(timestamp),\n  updatedAt: (timestamp) => timestamp?.toDate?.() || new Date(timestamp),\n}\n\n// PostGIS geometry to GeoJSON\nparse: {\n  location: (wkb: string) => parseWKBToGeoJSON(wkb)\n}\n\n// JSON string to object with error handling\nparse: {\n  metadata: (str: string) => {\n    try {\n      return JSON.parse(str)\n    } catch {\n      return {}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 5. Schemas and Type Transformations\n\nWhen building a custom collection, you need to decide how to handle the relationship between your backend's storage format and the client-side types users work with in their collections.\n\n#### Two Separate Concerns\n\n**Backend Format** - The types your storage layer uses (SQLite, Postgres, Firebase, etc.)\n- Examples: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, JSON strings, PostGIS geometries\n\n**Client Format** - The types users work with in their TanStack DB collections\n- Examples: Date objects, parsed JSON, GeoJSON\n\nSchemas in TanStack DB define the **client format** (TInput/TOutput for mutations). How you bridge between backend and client format depends on your integration design.\n\n#### Approach 1: Integration Provides Parse/Serialize Helpers\n\nFor backends with specific storage formats, provide `parse`/`serialize` options that users configure:\n\n```typescript\n// TrailBase example: User specifies field conversions\nexport function trailbaseCollectionOptions(config) {\n  return {\n    // ... getKey, schema, and sync options\n    // The sync implementation applies config.parse before calling write.\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const serialized = transaction.mutations.map(m =>\n        serializeFields(m.modified, config.serialize)\n      )\n      await config.recordApi.createBulk(serialized)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// User explicitly configures conversions\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  trailbaseCollectionOptions({\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    parse: {\n      created_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000)  // Unix → Date\n    },\n    serialize: {\n      created_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000)  // Date → Unix\n    }\n  })\n)\n```\n\n**Benefits:** Explicit control over type conversions. Integration handles applying them consistently.\n\n#### Approach 2: User Handles Everything in QueryFn/Handlers\n\nFor simple APIs or when users want full control, they handle parsing/serialization themselves:\n\n```typescript\n// Query Collection: User handles all transformations\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch('/api/todos')\n      const todos = await response.json()\n      // User manually parses to match their schema's TOutput\n      return todos.map(todo => ({\n        ...todo,\n        created_at: new Date(todo.created_at)  // ISO string → Date\n      }))\n    },\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // User manually serializes for their backend\n      await fetch('/api/todos', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify({\n          ...transaction.mutations[0].modified,\n          created_at: transaction.mutations[0].modified.created_at.toISOString()  // Date → ISO string\n        })\n      })\n    }\n  })\n)\n```\n\n**Benefits:** Maximum flexibility, no abstraction overhead. Users see exactly what's happening.\n\n#### Approach 3: Automatic Serialization in Handlers\n\nIf your backend has well-defined types, you can automatically serialize in mutation handlers:\n\n```typescript\nexport function myCollectionOptions(config) {\n  return {\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Automatically serialize known types for your backend\n      const serialized = transaction.mutations.map(m => ({\n        ...m.modified,\n        // Date objects → Unix timestamps for your backend\n        created_at: m.modified.created_at instanceof Date\n          ? Math.floor(m.modified.created_at.valueOf() / 1000)\n          : m.modified.created_at\n      }))\n      await backend.insert(serialized)\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:** Least configuration for users. Integration handles backend format automatically.\n\n#### Key Design Principles\n\n1. **Schemas validate client mutations only** - They don't affect how backend data is parsed during sync\n2. **TOutput is the application-facing type** - This is what users work with in their app\n3. **Choose your approach based on backend constraints** - Fixed types → automatic serialization; varying types → user configuration\n4. **Document your backend format clearly** - Explain what types your storage uses and how to handle them\n\nFor more on schemas from a user perspective, see the [Schemas guide](./schemas.md).\n\n### 6. Mutation Handler Patterns\n\nThere are two distinct patterns for handling mutations in collection options creators:\n\n#### Pattern A: User-Provided Handlers (Query, Standard)\n\nThe user provides mutation handlers in the config. Your collection creator passes them through.\n\n**Note:** Handler return values are deprecated. Users should trigger refetch/sync within their handlers.\n\n```typescript\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> {\n  // ... other config\n\n  // User provides these handlers\n  onInsert?: InsertMutationFn<TItem>\n  onUpdate?: UpdateMutationFn<TItem>\n  onDelete?: DeleteMutationFn<TItem>\n}\n\nexport function myCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: MyCollectionConfig<TItem>\n) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n    sync: {\n      sync: syncFn,\n      rowUpdateMode: config.rowUpdateMode ?? 'partial'\n    },\n\n    // Pass through user-provided handlers\n    // Users handle sync coordination in their own handlers\n    onInsert: config.onInsert,\n    onUpdate: config.onUpdate,\n    onDelete: config.onDelete\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n#### Pattern B: Built-in Handlers (Trailbase, WebSocket, Firebase)\n\nYour collection creator implements the handlers directly using the sync engine's APIs:\n\n```typescript\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object>\n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete'> {\n  // ... sync engine specific config\n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are NOT in the config\n}\n\nexport function myCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: MyCollectionConfig<TItem>\n) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n    sync: {\n      sync: syncFn,\n      rowUpdateMode: config.rowUpdateMode ?? 'partial'\n    },\n\n    // Implement handlers using sync engine APIs\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Handle provider-specific batch limits (e.g., Firestore's 500 limit)\n      const chunks = chunkArray(transaction.mutations, PROVIDER_BATCH_LIMIT)\n\n      for (const chunk of chunks) {\n        const ids = await config.recordApi.createBulk(\n          chunk.map(m => serialize(m.modified))\n        )\n        // Wait for these IDs to sync back before completing\n        await awaitIds(ids)\n      }\n      // Handler completes after sync coordination\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const chunks = chunkArray(transaction.mutations, PROVIDER_BATCH_LIMIT)\n\n      for (const chunk of chunks) {\n        await Promise.all(\n          chunk.map(m =>\n            config.recordApi.update(m.key, serialize(m.changes))\n          )\n        )\n      }\n\n      // Wait for mutations to sync back\n      await awaitIds(transaction.mutations.map(m => String(m.key)))\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nMany providers have batch size limits (Firestore: 500, DynamoDB: 25, etc.) so chunk large transactions accordingly.\n\n**Key Principle:** Built-in handlers should coordinate sync internally (using `awaitIds`, `awaitTxId`, or similar) and not rely on return values. The handler completes only after sync coordination is done.\n\nChoose Pattern A when users need to provide their own APIs, and Pattern B when your sync engine handles writes directly.\n\n## Row Update Modes\n\nCollections support two update modes:\n\n- **`partial`** (default) - Updates are merged with existing data\n- **`full`** - Updates replace the entire row\n\nConfigure this in your sync config:\n\n```typescript\nsync: {\n  sync: syncFn,\n  rowUpdateMode: 'full' // or 'partial'\n}\n```\n\n## Production Examples\n\nFor complete, production-ready examples, see the collection packages in the TanStack DB repository:\n\n- **[@tanstack/query-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/query-db-collection)** - Pattern A: User-provided handlers with full refetch strategy\n- **[@tanstack/trailbase-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/trailbase-db-collection)** - Pattern B: Built-in handlers with ID-based tracking\n- **[@tanstack/electric-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/electric-db-collection)** - Pattern A: Transaction ID tracking with complex sync protocols\n- **[@tanstack/rxdb-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/rxdb-db-collection)** - Pattern B: Built-in handlers that bridge [RxDB](https://rxdb.info) change streams into TanStack DB's sync lifecycle\n\n### Key Lessons from Production Collections\n\n**From Query Collection:**\n- Simplest approach: Full refetch after mutations\n- Best for: APIs without real-time sync\n- Pattern: User provides `onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete` handlers\n\n**From Trailbase Collection:**  \n- Shows ID-based optimistic state management\n- Handles provider batch limits (chunking large operations)\n- Pattern: Collection provides mutation handlers using record API\n\n**From Electric Collection:**\n- Complex transaction ID tracking for distributed sync\n- Demonstrates advanced deduplication techniques\n- Shows how to wrap user handlers with sync coordination\n\n**From RxDB Collection:**\n- Uses RxDB's built-in queries and change streams\n- Uses `RxCollection.$` to subscribe to inserts/updates/deletes and forward them to TanStack DB with begin-write-commit\n- Implements built-in mutation handlers (onInsert, onUpdate, onDelete) that call RxDB APIs (bulkUpsert, incrementalPatch, bulkRemove)\n\n## Complete Example: WebSocket Collection\n\nHere's a complete example of a WebSocket-based collection options creator that demonstrates the full round-trip flow:\n\n1. Client sends transaction with all mutations batched together\n2. Server processes the transaction and may modify the data (validation, timestamps, etc.)\n3. Server sends back acknowledgment and the actual processed data\n4. Client waits for this round-trip before dropping optimistic state\n\n```typescript\nimport type {\n  CollectionConfig,\n  SyncConfig,\n  InsertMutationFnParams,\n  UpdateMutationFnParams,\n  DeleteMutationFnParams,\n  UtilsRecord\n} from '@tanstack/db'\n\ninterface WebSocketMessage<T> {\n  type: 'insert' | 'update' | 'delete' | 'sync' | 'transaction' | 'ack'\n  data?: T | T[]\n  mutations?: Array<{\n    type: 'insert' | 'update' | 'delete'\n    data: T\n    id?: string\n  }>\n  transactionId?: string\n  id?: string\n}\n\ninterface WebSocketCollectionConfig<TItem extends object>\n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete' | 'sync'> {\n  url: string\n  reconnectInterval?: number\n  \n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are handled by the WebSocket connection\n  // Users don't provide these handlers\n}\n\ninterface WebSocketUtils extends UtilsRecord {\n  reconnect: () => void\n  getConnectionState: () => 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'connecting'\n}\n\nexport function webSocketCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: WebSocketCollectionConfig<TItem>\n): CollectionConfig<TItem> & { utils: WebSocketUtils } {\n  let ws: WebSocket | null = null\n  let reconnectTimer: NodeJS.Timeout | null = null\n  let connectionState: 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'connecting' = 'disconnected'\n  \n  // Track pending transactions awaiting acknowledgment\n  const pendingTransactions = new Map<string, {\n    resolve: () => void\n    reject: (error: Error) => void\n    timeout: NodeJS.Timeout\n  }>()\n  \n  const sync: SyncConfig<TItem>['sync'] = (params) => {\n    const { begin, write, commit, markReady } = params\n    \n    function connect() {\n      connectionState = 'connecting'\n      ws = new WebSocket(config.url)\n      \n      ws.onopen = () => {\n        connectionState = 'connected'\n        // Request initial sync\n        ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'sync' }))\n      }\n      \n      ws.onmessage = (event) => {\n        const message: WebSocketMessage<TItem> = JSON.parse(event.data)\n        \n        switch (message.type) {\n          case 'sync':\n            // Initial sync with array of items\n            begin()\n            if (Array.isArray(message.data)) {\n              for (const item of message.data) {\n                write({ type: 'insert', value: item })\n              }\n            }\n            commit()\n            markReady()\n            break\n            \n          case 'insert':\n          case 'update':\n          case 'delete':\n            // Real-time updates from other clients\n            begin()\n            write({ \n              type: message.type, \n              value: message.data as TItem \n            })\n            commit()\n            break\n            \n          case 'ack':\n            // Server acknowledged our transaction\n            if (message.transactionId) {\n              const pending = pendingTransactions.get(message.transactionId)\n              if (pending) {\n                clearTimeout(pending.timeout)\n                pendingTransactions.delete(message.transactionId)\n                pending.resolve()\n              }\n            }\n            break\n            \n          case 'transaction':\n            // Server sending back the actual data after processing our transaction\n            if (message.mutations) {\n              begin()\n              for (const mutation of message.mutations) {\n                write({\n                  type: mutation.type,\n                  value: mutation.data\n                })\n              }\n              commit()\n            }\n            break\n        }\n      }\n      \n      ws.onerror = (error) => {\n        console.error('WebSocket error:', error)\n        connectionState = 'disconnected'\n      }\n      \n      ws.onclose = () => {\n        connectionState = 'disconnected'\n        // Auto-reconnect\n        if (!reconnectTimer) {\n          reconnectTimer = setTimeout(() => {\n            reconnectTimer = null\n            connect()\n          }, config.reconnectInterval || 5000)\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Start connection\n    connect()\n    \n    // Return cleanup function\n    return () => {\n      if (reconnectTimer) {\n        clearTimeout(reconnectTimer)\n        reconnectTimer = null\n      }\n      if (ws) {\n        ws.close()\n        ws = null\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Helper function to send transaction and wait for server acknowledgment\n  const sendTransaction = async (\n    params: InsertMutationFnParams<TItem> | UpdateMutationFnParams<TItem> | DeleteMutationFnParams<TItem>\n  ): Promise<void> => {\n    if (ws?.readyState !== WebSocket.OPEN) {\n      throw new Error('WebSocket not connected')\n    }\n    \n    const transactionId = crypto.randomUUID()\n    \n    // Convert all mutations in the transaction to the wire format\n    const mutations = params.transaction.mutations.map(mutation => ({\n      type: mutation.type,\n      id: mutation.key,\n      data: mutation.type === 'delete' ? undefined : \n           mutation.type === 'update' ? mutation.changes : \n           mutation.modified\n    }))\n    \n    // Send the entire transaction at once\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({\n      type: 'transaction',\n      transactionId,\n      mutations\n    }))\n    \n    // Wait for server acknowledgment\n    return new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {\n      const timeout = setTimeout(() => {\n        pendingTransactions.delete(transactionId)\n        reject(new Error(`Transaction ${transactionId} timed out`))\n      }, 10000) // 10 second timeout\n      \n      pendingTransactions.set(transactionId, {\n        resolve,\n        reject,\n        timeout\n      })\n    })\n  }\n  \n  // All mutation handlers use the same transaction sender\n  // Handlers wait for server acknowledgment before completing\n  const onInsert = async (params: InsertMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n    // Handler completes after server confirms the transaction\n  }\n\n  const onUpdate = async (params: UpdateMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n  }\n\n  const onDelete = async (params: DeleteMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n  }\n  \n  return {\n    id: config.id,\n    schema: config.schema,\n    getKey: config.getKey,\n    sync: { sync },\n    onInsert,\n    onUpdate,\n    onDelete,\n    utils: {\n      reconnect: () => {\n        if (ws) ws.close()\n        connect()\n      },\n      getConnectionState: () => connectionState\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Usage Example\n\n```typescript\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { webSocketCollectionOptions } from './websocket-collection'\n\nconst db = new DbClient()\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions('todos', () =>\n  webSocketCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'todos',\n    url: 'ws://localhost:8080/todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    // Note: No onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete - handled by WebSocket automatically\n  })\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n\n// Use the collection\ntodos.insert({ id: '1', text: 'Buy milk', completed: false })\n\n// Access utilities\ntodos.utils.getConnectionState() // 'connected'\ntodos.utils.reconnect() // Force reconnect\n```\n\n## Advanced: Managing Optimistic State\n\nA critical challenge in sync-first apps is knowing when to drop optimistic state. When a user makes a change:\n\n1. The UI updates immediately (optimistic update)\n2. A mutation is sent to the backend\n3. The backend processes and persists the change\n4. The change syncs back to the client\n5. The optimistic state should be dropped in favor of the synced data\n\nThe key question is: **How do you know when step 4 is complete?**\n\n### Strategy 1: Built-in Provider Methods (Recommended)\n\nMany providers offer built-in methods to wait for sync completion:\n\n```typescript\n// Firebase\nawait waitForPendingWrites(firestore)\n\n// Custom WebSocket\nawait websocket.waitForAck(transactionId)\n```\n\n### Strategy 2: Transaction ID Tracking (ElectricSQL)\n\nElectricSQL returns transaction IDs that you can track:\n\n```typescript\n// Track seen transaction IDs\nconst seenTxids = new Store<Set<number>>(new Set())\n\n// In sync, track txids from incoming messages\nif (message.headers.txids) {\n  message.headers.txids.forEach(txid => {\n    seenTxids.setState(prev => new Set([...prev, txid]))\n  })\n}\n\n// Electric-specific: expose awaitTxId through collection.utils so handlers\n// can coordinate txid-based sync before they complete\nconst wrappedOnInsert = async (params) => {\n  // The user handler persists the mutation, then calls\n  // await params.collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid) internally.\n  await config.onInsert!(params)\n}\n\n// Utility function to wait for a txid\nconst awaitTxId = (txId: number): Promise<boolean> => {\n  if (seenTxids.state.has(txId)) return Promise.resolve(true)\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const unsubscribe = seenTxids.subscribe(() => {\n      if (seenTxids.state.has(txId)) {\n        unsubscribe()\n        resolve(true)\n      }\n    })\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Strategy 3: ID-Based Tracking (Trailbase)\n\nTrailbase tracks when specific record IDs have been synced:\n\n```typescript\n// Track synced IDs with timestamps\nconst seenIds = new Store(new Map<string, number>())\n\n// In sync, mark IDs as seen\nwrite({ type: 'insert', value: item })\nseenIds.setState(prev => new Map(prev).set(item.id, Date.now()))\n\n// Wait for specific IDs after mutations\nconst wrappedOnInsert = async (params) => {\n  const ids = await config.recordApi.createBulk(items)\n\n  // Wait for all IDs to be synced back before handler completes\n  await awaitIds(ids)\n}\n\nconst awaitIds = (ids: string[]): Promise<void> => {\n  const allSynced = ids.every(id => seenIds.state.has(id))\n  if (allSynced) return Promise.resolve()\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const unsubscribe = seenIds.subscribe((state) => {\n      if (ids.every(id => state.has(id))) {\n        unsubscribe()\n        resolve()\n      }\n    })\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Strategy 4: Version/Timestamp Tracking\n\nTrack version numbers or timestamps to detect when data is fresh:\n\n```typescript\n// Track latest sync timestamp\nlet lastSyncTime = 0\n\n// In mutations, record when the operation was sent\nconst wrappedOnUpdate = async (params) => {\n  const mutationTime = Date.now()\n  await config.onUpdate(params)\n\n  // Wait for sync to catch up before handler completes\n  await waitForSync(mutationTime)\n}\n\nconst waitForSync = (afterTime: number): Promise<void> => {\n  if (lastSyncTime > afterTime) return Promise.resolve()\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const check = setInterval(() => {\n      if (lastSyncTime > afterTime) {\n        clearInterval(check)\n        resolve()\n      }\n    }, 100)\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Strategy 5: Refetch (Query Collection)\n\nThe query collection pattern has users refetch after mutations:\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern A: User provides handlers and manages refetch\nexport function queryCollectionOptions<TItem>(config) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n\n    // User provides handlers and they handle refetch themselves\n    onInsert: config.onInsert,  // User calls collection.utils.refetch() in their handler\n    onUpdate: config.onUpdate,\n    onDelete: config.onDelete,\n\n    utils: {\n      refetch: () => {\n        // Refetch implementation that syncs fresh data\n        // automatically dropping optimistic state\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Usage: User refetches in their handler\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map(m => m.modified))\n      // User explicitly triggers refetch\n      await collection.utils.refetch()\n      // Prevent the pre-1.0 compatibility wrapper from refetching again.\n      return { refetch: false }\n    }\n  })\n)\n```\n\n### Choosing a Strategy\n\n- **Built-in Methods**: Best when your provider offers sync completion APIs\n- **Transaction IDs**: Best when your backend provides reliable transaction tracking\n- **ID-Based**: Good for systems where each mutation returns the affected IDs\n- **Full Refetch**: Simplest but least efficient; good for small datasets\n- **Version/Timestamp**: Works when your sync includes reliable ordering information\n\n### Implementation Tips\n\n1. **Always wait for sync** in your mutation handlers to ensure optimistic state is properly managed\n2. **Handle timeouts** - Don't wait forever for sync confirmation\n3. **Clean up tracking data** - Remove old txids/IDs to prevent memory leaks\n4. **Provide utilities** - Export functions like `awaitTxId` or `awaitSync` for advanced use cases\n\n## Best Practices\n\n1. **Report initial sync status** - Call `markReady()` after a usable snapshot, or `markError(error)` if initial sync fails\n2. **Recover explicitly** - After an error, call `markReady()` only when a later sync has produced a usable snapshot\n3. **Clean up resources** - Return a cleanup function from sync to prevent memory leaks\n4. **Batch operations** - Use begin/commit to batch multiple changes for better performance\n5. **Race Conditions** - Start listeners before initial fetch and buffer events\n6. **Type safety** - Use TypeScript generics to maintain type safety throughout\n7. **Provide utilities** - Export sync-engine-specific utilities for advanced use cases\n8. **Handler return values are deprecated** - Mutation handlers should coordinate sync internally (via `await`) rather than returning values. For Electric collections, use `await collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)` instead of returning the txid\n\n## Testing Your Collection\n\nTest your collection options creator with:\n\n1. **Unit tests** - Test sync logic, data transformations\n2. **Integration tests** - Test with real sync engine\n3. **Error scenarios** - Connection failures, invalid data\n4. **Performance** - Large datasets, frequent updates\n\n## Conclusion\n\nCreating a collection options creator allows you to integrate any sync engine with TanStack DB's powerful sync-first architecture. Follow the patterns shown here, and you'll have a robust, type-safe integration that provides excellent developer experience.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Creating a Collection Options Creator\nid: guide/collection-options-creator\n---\nA collection options creator is a factory function that generates configuration options for TanStack DB collections. It provides a standardized way to integrate different sync engines and data sources with TanStack DB's reactive sync-first architecture.\n\n## Overview\n\nCollection options creators follow a consistent pattern:\n1. Accept configuration specific to the sync engine\n2. Return an object that satisfies the `CollectionConfig` interface\n3. Handle sync initialization, data parsing, and transaction management\n4. Optionally provide utility functions specific to the sync engine\n\n## When to Create a Custom Collection\n\nYou should create a custom collection when:\n- You have a dedicated sync engine (like ElectricSQL, Trailbase, Firebase, RxDB or a custom WebSocket solution)\n- You need specific sync behaviors that aren't covered by the query collection\n- You want to integrate with a backend that has its own sync protocol\n\n**Note**: If you're just hitting an API and returning data, use the query collection instead.\n\n## Core Requirements\n\nEvery collection options creator must implement these key responsibilities:\n\n### 1. Configuration Interface\n\nDefine a configuration interface that extends or includes standard collection properties:\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern A: User provides handlers (Query / ElectricSQL style)\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> {\n  // Your sync engine specific options\n  connectionUrl: string\n  apiKey?: string\n  \n  // Standard collection properties\n  id?: string\n  schema?: StandardSchemaV1\n  getKey: (item: TItem) => string | number\n  sync?: SyncConfig<TItem>\n  \n  rowUpdateMode?: 'partial' | 'full'\n  \n  // User provides mutation handlers\n  onInsert?: InsertMutationFn<TItem>\n  onUpdate?: UpdateMutationFn<TItem>\n  onDelete?: DeleteMutationFn<TItem>\n}\n\n// Pattern B: Built-in handlers (Trailbase style)\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> \n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete'> {\n  // Your sync engine specific options\n  recordApi: MyRecordApi<TItem>\n  connectionUrl: string\n  \n  rowUpdateMode?: 'partial' | 'full'\n  \n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are implemented by your collection creator\n}\n```\n\nAdapter-specific properties can be returned alongside the core options. TanStack DB ignores these extra properties. Development builds warn only about likely misspellings of a core option, such as `oninsert` for `onInsert`, when the correctly named option is absent. Missing or invalid required core options still throw.\n\nKeep adapter behavior in the adapter: returning `parse` or `serialize` does not make TanStack DB apply these conversions. Apply `parse` before calling `write` in your sync implementation, and apply `serialize` in mutation handlers. Put `rowUpdateMode` inside the returned `sync` object.\n\n### 2. Sync Implementation\n\nEach call to the sync function starts a **sync run**. The run owns the callbacks\nand resources installed by that call until its returned cleanup ends them. A\nsync run may make several backend requests or open a longer-lived provider\nsession, so it is not itself a request or provider session.\n\nThe sync function is the heart of your collection. It must return a cleanup\nfunction for proper garbage collection:\n\n```typescript\nconst sync: SyncConfig<T>['sync'] = (params) => {\n  const { begin, write, commit, markReady, markError, collection } = params\n  \n  // 1. Initialize connection to your sync engine\n  const connection = initializeConnection(config)\n  const initialSyncAbort = new AbortController()\n  \n  // 2. Set up real-time subscription FIRST (prevents race conditions)\n  const eventBuffer: Array<any> = []\n  let isInitialSyncComplete = false\n  \n  connection.subscribe((event) => {\n    if (!isInitialSyncComplete) {\n      // Buffer events during initial sync to prevent race conditions\n      eventBuffer.push(event)\n      return\n    }\n    \n    // Process real-time events\n    begin()\n    \n    switch (event.type) {\n      case 'insert':\n        write({ type: 'insert', value: event.data })\n        break\n      case 'update':\n        write({ type: 'update', value: event.data })\n        break\n      case 'delete':\n        write({ type: 'delete', value: event.data })\n        break\n    }\n    \n    commit()\n  })\n  \n  // 3. Perform initial data fetch\n  async function initialSync() {\n    try {\n      const data = await fetchInitialData({ signal: initialSyncAbort.signal })\n      \n      begin() // Start a transaction\n      \n      for (const item of data) {\n        write({\n          type: 'insert',\n          value: item\n        })\n      }\n      \n      commit() // Commit the transaction\n      \n      // 4. Process buffered events\n      isInitialSyncComplete = true\n      if (eventBuffer.length > 0) {\n        begin()\n        for (const event of eventBuffer) {\n          // Deduplicate if necessary based on your sync engine\n          write({ type: event.type, value: event.data })\n        }\n        commit()\n        eventBuffer.splice(0)\n      }\n\n      // A complete initial snapshot is now available.\n      markReady()\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (initialSyncAbort.signal.aborted) return\n      console.error('Initial sync failed:', error)\n      // No usable initial snapshot exists.\n      // Only initial startup owns collection readiness. A later refetch\n      // failure must keep the last ready snapshot usable.\n      if (collection.status === 'loading') markError(error)\n    }\n  }\n\n  initialSync()\n  \n  // 4. Return cleanup function\n  return () => {\n    initialSyncAbort.abort()\n    connection.close()\n    // Clean up any timers, intervals, or other resources\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Transaction Lifecycle\n\nUnderstanding the transaction lifecycle is important for correct implementation.\n\nThe sync process follows this lifecycle:\n\n1. **begin()** - Start collecting changes\n2. **write()** - Add changes to the pending transaction (buffered until commit)\n3. **commit()** - Apply all changes atomically to the collection state\n4. **markReady()** - Signal that a usable initial or recovered snapshot exists\n5. **markError(error?)** - Signal that initial sync failed before producing a usable snapshot; pass the cause so readiness waits reject with it\n\n`commit()` accepts the transaction and returns `true`. An accepted transaction\nalways applies, in commit order. While an optimistic transaction is persisting,\nthe accepted transaction waits. It becomes visible when that optimistic\ntransaction settles, in the same publication that drops its optimistic state.\nA mutation handler can therefore await its own write without waiting for\nitself. A wrapping sync, such as persistence, returns a promise that resolves\nafter its durable step. A successful `loadSubset` must await or return every\ncommit receipt that establishes its result.\n\nThe collection keeps the object you pass to `write()` as the row's stored\nvalue. Pass a new object for each update. If your source changes a row object\nin place and writes it again, pass the row's previous value as\n`previousValue`. Without it, the change already overwrote the value the\ncollection needs to publish the update, and live queries can keep the row in\na result it left. In development, the collection throws\n`SyncRowReusedWithoutPreviousValueError` for that write when a top-level field\nchanged. The check compares shallow copies, so it does not detect a change\ninside a nested object. Pass `previousValue` for those writes too.\n\n```ts\n// Changes a stored row in place, so it must name the previous value\nconst previousValue = { ...row }\nrow.status = `done`\nwrite({ type: `update`, value: row, previousValue })\n```\n\nFor request-scoped writes, pass the request's abort signal to `commit(signal)`.\nIf the signal has already aborted, the transaction is abandoned and the receipt\nrejects with `AbortError`. An accepted transaction ignores a later abort: its\nrows apply. To discard a stale page, check the signal before `commit()`. If the\ncaller aborted after the page was accepted, the rows still apply, but reject the\nload with `AbortError` once its receipt resolves. Do not attach one request's\nsignal to a shared stream transaction.\n\nIf an adapter supplies `unloadSubset`, release only the acquisition belonging to\nthe supplied options. Release must be idempotent and non-throwing; the adapter\nowns any remote unsubscribe retry. A synchronous `loadSubset` throw must clean\nup resources acquired before it throws. Returning a promise transfers ownership\neven if that promise later rejects, so failed acquisitions must remain safe to\nrelease without affecting peers.\n\n**Race Condition Prevention:**\nMany sync engines start real-time subscriptions before the initial sync completes. Your implementation MUST deduplicate events that arrive via subscription that represent the same data as the initial sync. Consider:\n- Starting the listener BEFORE initial fetch and buffering events\n- Tracking timestamps, sequence numbers, or document versions\n- Using read timestamps or other ordering mechanisms\n\n### 4. Data Parsing and Type Conversion\n\nIf your sync engine returns data with different types, provide conversion functions for specific fields:\n\n```typescript\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem, TRecord> {\n  // ... other config\n  \n  // Only specify conversions for fields that need type conversion\n  parse: {\n    created_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000),  // timestamp -> Date\n    updated_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000),  // timestamp -> Date\n    metadata?: (str: string) => JSON.parse(str)       // JSON string -> object\n  }\n  \n  serialize: {\n    created_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000),  // Date -> timestamp\n    updated_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000),  // Date -> timestamp  \n    metadata?: (obj: object) => JSON.stringify(obj)                 // object -> JSON string\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Type Conversion Examples:**\n```typescript\n// Firebase Timestamp to Date\nparse: {\n  createdAt: (timestamp) => timestamp?.toDate?.() || new Date(timestamp),\n  updatedAt: (timestamp) => timestamp?.toDate?.() || new Date(timestamp),\n}\n\n// PostGIS geometry to GeoJSON\nparse: {\n  location: (wkb: string) => parseWKBToGeoJSON(wkb)\n}\n\n// JSON string to object with error handling\nparse: {\n  metadata: (str: string) => {\n    try {\n      return JSON.parse(str)\n    } catch {\n      return {}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 5. Schemas and Type Transformations\n\nWhen building a custom collection, you need to decide how to handle the relationship between your backend's storage format and the client-side types users work with in their collections.\n\n#### Two Separate Concerns\n\n**Backend Format** - The types your storage layer uses (SQLite, Postgres, Firebase, etc.)\n- Examples: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, JSON strings, PostGIS geometries\n\n**Client Format** - The types users work with in their TanStack DB collections\n- Examples: Date objects, parsed JSON, GeoJSON\n\nSchemas in TanStack DB define the **client format** (TInput/TOutput for mutations). How you bridge between backend and client format depends on your integration design.\n\n#### Approach 1: Integration Provides Parse/Serialize Helpers\n\nFor backends with specific storage formats, provide `parse`/`serialize` options that users configure:\n\n```typescript\n// TrailBase example: User specifies field conversions\nexport function trailbaseCollectionOptions(config) {\n  return {\n    // ... getKey, schema, and sync options\n    // The sync implementation applies config.parse before calling write.\n\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const serialized = transaction.mutations.map(m =>\n        serializeFields(m.modified, config.serialize)\n      )\n      await config.recordApi.createBulk(serialized)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// User explicitly configures conversions\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  trailbaseCollectionOptions({\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    parse: {\n      created_at: (ts: number) => new Date(ts * 1000)  // Unix → Date\n    },\n    serialize: {\n      created_at: (date: Date) => Math.floor(date.valueOf() / 1000)  // Date → Unix\n    }\n  })\n)\n```\n\n**Benefits:** Explicit control over type conversions. Integration handles applying them consistently.\n\n#### Approach 2: User Handles Everything in QueryFn/Handlers\n\nFor simple APIs or when users want full control, they handle parsing/serialization themselves:\n\n```typescript\n// Query Collection: User handles all transformations\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    queryFn: async () => {\n      const response = await fetch('/api/todos')\n      const todos = await response.json()\n      // User manually parses to match their schema's TOutput\n      return todos.map(todo => ({\n        ...todo,\n        created_at: new Date(todo.created_at)  // ISO string → Date\n      }))\n    },\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // User manually serializes for their backend\n      await fetch('/api/todos', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify({\n          ...transaction.mutations[0].modified,\n          created_at: transaction.mutations[0].modified.created_at.toISOString()  // Date → ISO string\n        })\n      })\n    }\n  })\n)\n```\n\n**Benefits:** Maximum flexibility, no abstraction overhead. Users see exactly what's happening.\n\n#### Approach 3: Automatic Serialization in Handlers\n\nIf your backend has well-defined types, you can automatically serialize in mutation handlers:\n\n```typescript\nexport function myCollectionOptions(config) {\n  return {\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Automatically serialize known types for your backend\n      const serialized = transaction.mutations.map(m => ({\n        ...m.modified,\n        // Date objects → Unix timestamps for your backend\n        created_at: m.modified.created_at instanceof Date\n          ? Math.floor(m.modified.created_at.valueOf() / 1000)\n          : m.modified.created_at\n      }))\n      await backend.insert(serialized)\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:** Least configuration for users. Integration handles backend format automatically.\n\n#### Key Design Principles\n\n1. **Schemas validate client mutations only** - They don't affect how backend data is parsed during sync\n2. **TOutput is the application-facing type** - This is what users work with in their app\n3. **Choose your approach based on backend constraints** - Fixed types → automatic serialization; varying types → user configuration\n4. **Document your backend format clearly** - Explain what types your storage uses and how to handle them\n\nFor more on schemas from a user perspective, see the [Schemas guide](./schemas.md).\n\n### 6. Mutation Handler Patterns\n\nThere are two distinct patterns for handling mutations in collection options creators:\n\n#### Pattern A: User-Provided Handlers (Query, Standard)\n\nThe user provides mutation handlers in the config. Your collection creator passes them through.\n\n**Note:** Handler return values are deprecated. Users should trigger refetch/sync within their handlers.\n\n```typescript\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object> {\n  // ... other config\n\n  // User provides these handlers\n  onInsert?: InsertMutationFn<TItem>\n  onUpdate?: UpdateMutationFn<TItem>\n  onDelete?: DeleteMutationFn<TItem>\n}\n\nexport function myCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: MyCollectionConfig<TItem>\n) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n    sync: {\n      sync: syncFn,\n      rowUpdateMode: config.rowUpdateMode ?? 'partial'\n    },\n\n    // Pass through user-provided handlers\n    // Users handle sync coordination in their own handlers\n    onInsert: config.onInsert,\n    onUpdate: config.onUpdate,\n    onDelete: config.onDelete\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n#### Pattern B: Built-in Handlers (Trailbase, WebSocket, Firebase)\n\nYour collection creator implements the handlers directly using the sync engine's APIs:\n\n```typescript\ninterface MyCollectionConfig<TItem extends object>\n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete'> {\n  // ... sync engine specific config\n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are NOT in the config\n}\n\nexport function myCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: MyCollectionConfig<TItem>\n) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n    sync: {\n      sync: syncFn,\n      rowUpdateMode: config.rowUpdateMode ?? 'partial'\n    },\n\n    // Implement handlers using sync engine APIs\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Handle provider-specific batch limits (e.g., Firestore's 500 limit)\n      const chunks = chunkArray(transaction.mutations, PROVIDER_BATCH_LIMIT)\n\n      for (const chunk of chunks) {\n        const ids = await config.recordApi.createBulk(\n          chunk.map(m => serialize(m.modified))\n        )\n        // Wait for these IDs to sync back before completing\n        await awaitIds(ids)\n      }\n      // Handler completes after sync coordination\n    },\n\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      const chunks = chunkArray(transaction.mutations, PROVIDER_BATCH_LIMIT)\n\n      for (const chunk of chunks) {\n        await Promise.all(\n          chunk.map(m =>\n            config.recordApi.update(m.key, serialize(m.changes))\n          )\n        )\n      }\n\n      // Wait for mutations to sync back\n      await awaitIds(transaction.mutations.map(m => String(m.key)))\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nMany providers have batch size limits (Firestore: 500, DynamoDB: 25, etc.) so chunk large transactions accordingly.\n\n**Key Principle:** Built-in handlers should coordinate sync internally (using `awaitIds`, `awaitTxId`, or similar) and not rely on return values. The handler completes only after sync coordination is done.\n\nChoose Pattern A when users need to provide their own APIs, and Pattern B when your sync engine handles writes directly.\n\n## Row Update Modes\n\nCollections support two update modes:\n\n- **`partial`** (default) - Updates are merged with existing data\n- **`full`** - Updates replace the entire row\n\nConfigure this in your sync config:\n\n```typescript\nsync: {\n  sync: syncFn,\n  rowUpdateMode: 'full' // or 'partial'\n}\n```\n\n## Production Examples\n\nFor complete, production-ready examples, see the collection packages in the TanStack DB repository:\n\n- **[@tanstack/query-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/query-db-collection)** - Pattern A: User-provided handlers with full refetch strategy\n- **[@tanstack/trailbase-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/trailbase-db-collection)** - Pattern B: Built-in handlers with ID-based tracking\n- **[@tanstack/electric-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/electric-db-collection)** - Pattern A: Transaction ID tracking with complex sync protocols\n- **[@tanstack/rxdb-db-collection](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/rxdb-db-collection)** - Pattern B: Built-in handlers that bridge [RxDB](https://rxdb.info) change streams into TanStack DB's sync lifecycle\n\n### Key Lessons from Production Collections\n\n**From Query Collection:**\n- Simplest approach: Full refetch after mutations\n- Best for: APIs without real-time sync\n- Pattern: User provides `onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete` handlers\n\n**From Trailbase Collection:**  \n- Shows ID-based optimistic state management\n- Handles provider batch limits (chunking large operations)\n- Pattern: Collection provides mutation handlers using record API\n\n**From Electric Collection:**\n- Complex transaction ID tracking for distributed sync\n- Demonstrates advanced deduplication techniques\n- Shows how to wrap user handlers with sync coordination\n\n**From RxDB Collection:**\n- Uses RxDB's built-in queries and change streams\n- Uses `RxCollection.$` to subscribe to inserts/updates/deletes and forward them to TanStack DB with begin-write-commit\n- Implements built-in mutation handlers (onInsert, onUpdate, onDelete) that call RxDB APIs (bulkUpsert, incrementalPatch, bulkRemove)\n\n## Complete Example: WebSocket Collection\n\nHere's a complete example of a WebSocket-based collection options creator that demonstrates the full round-trip flow:\n\n1. Client sends transaction with all mutations batched together\n2. Server processes the transaction and may modify the data (validation, timestamps, etc.)\n3. Server sends back acknowledgment and the actual processed data\n4. Client waits for this round-trip before dropping optimistic state\n\n```typescript\nimport type {\n  CollectionConfig,\n  SyncConfig,\n  InsertMutationFnParams,\n  UpdateMutationFnParams,\n  DeleteMutationFnParams,\n  UtilsRecord\n} from '@tanstack/db'\n\ninterface WebSocketMessage<T> {\n  type: 'insert' | 'update' | 'delete' | 'sync' | 'transaction' | 'ack'\n  data?: T | T[]\n  mutations?: Array<{\n    type: 'insert' | 'update' | 'delete'\n    data: T\n    id?: string\n  }>\n  transactionId?: string\n  id?: string\n}\n\ninterface WebSocketCollectionConfig<TItem extends object>\n  extends Omit<CollectionConfig<TItem>, 'onInsert' | 'onUpdate' | 'onDelete' | 'sync'> {\n  url: string\n  reconnectInterval?: number\n  \n  // Note: onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete are handled by the WebSocket connection\n  // Users don't provide these handlers\n}\n\ninterface WebSocketUtils extends UtilsRecord {\n  reconnect: () => void\n  getConnectionState: () => 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'connecting'\n}\n\nexport function webSocketCollectionOptions<TItem extends object>(\n  config: WebSocketCollectionConfig<TItem>\n): CollectionConfig<TItem> & { utils: WebSocketUtils } {\n  let ws: WebSocket | null = null\n  let reconnectTimer: NodeJS.Timeout | null = null\n  let connectionState: 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'connecting' = 'disconnected'\n  \n  // Track pending transactions awaiting acknowledgment\n  const pendingTransactions = new Map<string, {\n    resolve: () => void\n    reject: (error: Error) => void\n    timeout: NodeJS.Timeout\n  }>()\n  \n  const sync: SyncConfig<TItem>['sync'] = (params) => {\n    const { begin, write, commit, markReady } = params\n    \n    function connect() {\n      connectionState = 'connecting'\n      ws = new WebSocket(config.url)\n      \n      ws.onopen = () => {\n        connectionState = 'connected'\n        // Request initial sync\n        ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'sync' }))\n      }\n      \n      ws.onmessage = (event) => {\n        const message: WebSocketMessage<TItem> = JSON.parse(event.data)\n        \n        switch (message.type) {\n          case 'sync':\n            // Initial sync with array of items\n            begin()\n            if (Array.isArray(message.data)) {\n              for (const item of message.data) {\n                write({ type: 'insert', value: item })\n              }\n            }\n            commit()\n            markReady()\n            break\n            \n          case 'insert':\n          case 'update':\n          case 'delete':\n            // Real-time updates from other clients\n            begin()\n            write({ \n              type: message.type, \n              value: message.data as TItem \n            })\n            commit()\n            break\n            \n          case 'ack':\n            // Server acknowledged our transaction\n            if (message.transactionId) {\n              const pending = pendingTransactions.get(message.transactionId)\n              if (pending) {\n                clearTimeout(pending.timeout)\n                pendingTransactions.delete(message.transactionId)\n                pending.resolve()\n              }\n            }\n            break\n            \n          case 'transaction':\n            // Server sending back the actual data after processing our transaction\n            if (message.mutations) {\n              begin()\n              for (const mutation of message.mutations) {\n                write({\n                  type: mutation.type,\n                  value: mutation.data\n                })\n              }\n              commit()\n            }\n            break\n        }\n      }\n      \n      ws.onerror = (error) => {\n        console.error('WebSocket error:', error)\n        connectionState = 'disconnected'\n      }\n      \n      ws.onclose = () => {\n        connectionState = 'disconnected'\n        // Auto-reconnect\n        if (!reconnectTimer) {\n          reconnectTimer = setTimeout(() => {\n            reconnectTimer = null\n            connect()\n          }, config.reconnectInterval || 5000)\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Start connection\n    connect()\n    \n    // Return cleanup function\n    return () => {\n      if (reconnectTimer) {\n        clearTimeout(reconnectTimer)\n        reconnectTimer = null\n      }\n      if (ws) {\n        ws.close()\n        ws = null\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Helper function to send transaction and wait for server acknowledgment\n  const sendTransaction = async (\n    params: InsertMutationFnParams<TItem> | UpdateMutationFnParams<TItem> | DeleteMutationFnParams<TItem>\n  ): Promise<void> => {\n    if (ws?.readyState !== WebSocket.OPEN) {\n      throw new Error('WebSocket not connected')\n    }\n    \n    const transactionId = crypto.randomUUID()\n    \n    // Convert all mutations in the transaction to the wire format\n    const mutations = params.transaction.mutations.map(mutation => ({\n      type: mutation.type,\n      id: mutation.key,\n      data: mutation.type === 'delete' ? undefined : \n           mutation.type === 'update' ? mutation.changes : \n           mutation.modified\n    }))\n    \n    // Send the entire transaction at once\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({\n      type: 'transaction',\n      transactionId,\n      mutations\n    }))\n    \n    // Wait for server acknowledgment\n    return new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {\n      const timeout = setTimeout(() => {\n        pendingTransactions.delete(transactionId)\n        reject(new Error(`Transaction ${transactionId} timed out`))\n      }, 10000) // 10 second timeout\n      \n      pendingTransactions.set(transactionId, {\n        resolve,\n        reject,\n        timeout\n      })\n    })\n  }\n  \n  // All mutation handlers use the same transaction sender\n  // Handlers wait for server acknowledgment before completing\n  const onInsert = async (params: InsertMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n    // Handler completes after server confirms the transaction\n  }\n\n  const onUpdate = async (params: UpdateMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n  }\n\n  const onDelete = async (params: DeleteMutationFnParams<TItem>): Promise<void> => {\n    await sendTransaction(params)\n  }\n  \n  return {\n    id: config.id,\n    schema: config.schema,\n    getKey: config.getKey,\n    sync: { sync },\n    onInsert,\n    onUpdate,\n    onDelete,\n    utils: {\n      reconnect: () => {\n        if (ws) ws.close()\n        connect()\n      },\n      getConnectionState: () => connectionState\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Usage Example\n\n```typescript\nimport { DbClient, collectionOptions } from '@tanstack/react-db'\nimport { webSocketCollectionOptions } from './websocket-collection'\n\nconst db = new DbClient()\n\nconst todosCollection = collectionOptions('todos', () =>\n  webSocketCollectionOptions({\n    id: 'todos',\n    url: 'ws://localhost:8080/todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    // Note: No onInsert/onUpdate/onDelete - handled by WebSocket automatically\n  })\n)\n\nconst todos = db.collection(todosCollection)\n\n// Use the collection\ntodos.insert({ id: '1', text: 'Buy milk', completed: false })\n\n// Access utilities\ntodos.utils.getConnectionState() // 'connected'\ntodos.utils.reconnect() // Force reconnect\n```\n\n## Advanced: Managing Optimistic State\n\nA critical challenge in sync-first apps is knowing when to drop optimistic state. When a user makes a change:\n\n1. The UI updates immediately (optimistic update)\n2. A mutation is sent to the backend\n3. The backend processes and persists the change\n4. The change syncs back to the client\n5. The optimistic state should be dropped in favor of the synced data\n\nThe key question is: **How do you know when step 4 is complete?**\n\n### Strategy 1: Built-in Provider Methods (Recommended)\n\nMany providers offer built-in methods to wait for sync completion:\n\n```typescript\n// Firebase\nawait waitForPendingWrites(firestore)\n\n// Custom WebSocket\nawait websocket.waitForAck(transactionId)\n```\n\n### Strategy 2: Transaction ID Tracking (ElectricSQL)\n\nElectricSQL returns transaction IDs that you can track:\n\n```typescript\n// Track seen transaction IDs\nconst seenTxids = new Store<Set<number>>(new Set())\n\n// In sync, track txids from incoming messages\nif (message.headers.txids) {\n  message.headers.txids.forEach(txid => {\n    seenTxids.setState(prev => new Set([...prev, txid]))\n  })\n}\n\n// Electric-specific: expose awaitTxId through collection.utils so handlers\n// can coordinate txid-based sync before they complete\nconst wrappedOnInsert = async (params) => {\n  // The user handler persists the mutation, then calls\n  // await params.collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid) internally.\n  await config.onInsert!(params)\n}\n\n// Utility function to wait for a txid\nconst awaitTxId = (txId: number): Promise<boolean> => {\n  if (seenTxids.state.has(txId)) return Promise.resolve(true)\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const unsubscribe = seenTxids.subscribe(() => {\n      if (seenTxids.state.has(txId)) {\n        unsubscribe()\n        resolve(true)\n      }\n    })\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Strategy 3: ID-Based Tracking (Trailbase)\n\nTrailbase tracks when specific record IDs have been synced:\n\n```typescript\n// Track synced IDs with timestamps\nconst seenIds = new Store(new Map<string, number>())\n\n// In sync, mark IDs as seen\nwrite({ type: 'insert', value: item })\nseenIds.setState(prev => new Map(prev).set(item.id, Date.now()))\n\n// Wait for specific IDs after mutations\nconst wrappedOnInsert = async (params) => {\n  const ids = await config.recordApi.createBulk(items)\n\n  // Wait for all IDs to be synced back before handler completes\n  await awaitIds(ids)\n}\n\nconst awaitIds = (ids: string[]): Promise<void> => {\n  const allSynced = ids.every(id => seenIds.state.has(id))\n  if (allSynced) return Promise.resolve()\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const unsubscribe = seenIds.subscribe((state) => {\n      if (ids.every(id => state.has(id))) {\n        unsubscribe()\n        resolve()\n      }\n    })\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Strategy 4: Version/Timestamp Tracking\n\nTrack version numbers or timestamps to detect when data is fresh:\n\n```typescript\n// Track latest sync timestamp\nlet lastSyncTime = 0\n\n// In mutations, record when the operation was sent\nconst wrappedOnUpdate = async (params) => {\n  const mutationTime = Date.now()\n  await config.onUpdate(params)\n\n  // Wait for sync to catch up before handler completes\n  await waitForSync(mutationTime)\n}\n\nconst waitForSync = (afterTime: number): Promise<void> => {\n  if (lastSyncTime > afterTime) return Promise.resolve()\n  \n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    const check = setInterval(() => {\n      if (lastSyncTime > afterTime) {\n        clearInterval(check)\n        resolve()\n      }\n    }, 100)\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Strategy 5: Refetch (Query Collection)\n\nThe query collection pattern has users refetch after mutations:\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern A: User provides handlers and manages refetch\nexport function queryCollectionOptions<TItem>(config) {\n  return {\n    // ... other options\n\n    // User provides handlers and they handle refetch themselves\n    onInsert: config.onInsert,  // User calls collection.utils.refetch() in their handler\n    onUpdate: config.onUpdate,\n    onDelete: config.onDelete,\n\n    utils: {\n      refetch: () => {\n        // Refetch implementation that syncs fresh data\n        // automatically dropping optimistic state\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Usage: User refetches in their handler\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n      await api.createTodos(transaction.mutations.map(m => m.modified))\n      // User explicitly triggers refetch\n      await collection.utils.refetch()\n      // Prevent the pre-1.0 compatibility wrapper from refetching again.\n      return { refetch: false }\n    }\n  })\n)\n```\n\n### Choosing a Strategy\n\n- **Built-in Methods**: Best when your provider offers sync completion APIs\n- **Transaction IDs**: Best when your backend provides reliable transaction tracking\n- **ID-Based**: Good for systems where each mutation returns the affected IDs\n- **Full Refetch**: Simplest but least efficient; good for small datasets\n- **Version/Timestamp**: Works when your sync includes reliable ordering information\n\n### Implementation Tips\n\n1. **Always wait for sync** in your mutation handlers to ensure optimistic state is properly managed\n2. **Handle timeouts** - Don't wait forever for sync confirmation\n3. **Clean up tracking data** - Remove old txids/IDs to prevent memory leaks\n4. **Provide utilities** - Export functions like `awaitTxId` or `awaitSync` for advanced use cases\n\n## Best Practices\n\n1. **Report initial sync status** - Call `markReady()` after a usable snapshot, or `markError(error)` if initial sync fails\n2. **Recover explicitly** - After an error, call `markReady()` only when a later sync has produced a usable snapshot\n3. **Clean up resources** - Return a cleanup function from sync to prevent memory leaks\n4. **Batch operations** - Use begin/commit to batch multiple changes for better performance\n5. **Race Conditions** - Start listeners before initial fetch and buffer events\n6. **Type safety** - Use TypeScript generics to maintain type safety throughout\n7. **Provide utilities** - Export sync-engine-specific utilities for advanced use cases\n8. **Handler return values are deprecated** - Mutation handlers should coordinate sync internally (via `await`) rather than returning values. For Electric collections, use `await collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)` instead of returning the txid\n\n## Testing Your Collection\n\nTest your collection options creator with:\n\n1. **Unit tests** - Test sync logic, data transformations\n2. **Integration tests** - Test with real sync engine\n3. **Error scenarios** - Connection failures, invalid data\n4. **Performance** - Large datasets, frequent updates\n\n## Conclusion\n\nCreating a collection options creator allows you to integrate any sync engine with TanStack DB's powerful sync-first architecture. Follow the patterns shown here, and you'll have a robust, type-safe integration that provides excellent developer experience.\n"}
